 Nearest Neighbor Sampling is a voxel interpolation technique that retrieves the value of the single voxel whose integer coordinates are closest to the ray's current position. It serves as a fallback or low-cost alternative when higher-order interpolation is not required or when computational resources are constrained. Unlike trilinear interpolation, which combines neighboring samples to produce smoother results, Nearest Neighbor Sampling selects only the closest sample, resulting in lower image quality but significantly reduced execution time.
 
+### [Volumetric Ray Casting with Trilinear Interpolation](13406-volumetric-ray-casting-with-trilinear-interpolation.md)
+Volumetric Ray Casting with Trilinear Interpolation is a direct volume rendering technique that visualizes scalar data stored in a voxel grid by casting rays through the volume and interpolating values at sample points using the 8 corners of the enclosing voxel cell. It differs from higher-order methods by using a simple, computationally inexpensive interpolation kernel that results in blocky artifacts but requires minimal memory and processing overhead.
