 ## Background
-Interpolation is a fundamental component of direct volume ray casting, introduced alongside early ray casting implementations to handle the mismatch between discrete voxel grids and continuous ray positions [S1, unofficial]. Nearest neighbor and trilinear interpolation were early standard methods, with trilinear offering improved image quality at the cost of substantial computational effort [S1, unofficial]. Forward projection methods like Joseph's method (1982) and Köhler's method (2000) were developed to improve accuracy in projection-based rendering, with Köhler separating volume construction from line-integration [S2, unofficial]. Shape-Based Interpolation emerged to address geometric fidelity issues in surface rendering [S1, unofficial]. Lanczos resampling was adopted for its superior frequency response compared to cubic interpolation [S1, unofficial]. Gradient-based splatting was developed to handle complex lighting in inhomogeneous media where standard interpolation fails [S1, unofficial]. Interblock interpolation was introduced in the context of GPU-based volume ray-casting with advanced illumination to manage sparse data [S3, unofficial]. Higher-order template interpolation extended template-based rendering to support continuous value variation within cells [S4, unofficial].
+Interpolation is fundamental to volume rendering, bridging the gap between discrete voxel data and continuous ray sampling. Early methods relied on nearest-neighbor sampling due to its simplicity, but this introduced significant aliasing and staircase artifacts [S1, unofficial]. The introduction of trilinear interpolation provided a smoother visual result at the cost of increased computational effort [S1, unofficial]. Joseph's method (1982) and Köhler's method (2000) refined forward projection techniques, with Köhler's approach separating volume construction from line integration using tri-linear interpolation [S3, unofficial]. Template-based ray casting was later extended to support higher-order interpolation by utilizing connected cells and precomputed interpolation tables [S4, unofficial]. Modern techniques like Lanczos resampling and gradient-based splatting address specific needs for sharpness and accurate shading in complex media [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
